\value{
\code{print()} returns \code{x}, invisibly.
\code{tidy()} and \code{glance()} return a tibble.
\code{tidy()} gives the columns \code{term}, \code{estimate}, \code{statistic}, and
\code{p.value} for a regression.
For a \code{netreg}, it also adds the columns of the fit's term table,
\code{fit$terms}: the \code{effect} (the formula term) each coefficient comes from,
its \code{layer}, \code{attribute}, \code{category}, and \code{reference} category,
and for an interaction its \code{operands}.
These group the coefficients without parsing their names.
and \code{glance()} gives the fit statistics that the model keeps.
For a \code{network_test}, both give one row with the observed statistic,
the mean and standard deviation of the null distribution,
and the share of the null distribution at or below (\code{p.lower})
and at or above (\code{p.upper}) the observed statistic.
For \code{test_gof()} and \code{test_distributions()}, the statistic is a distance,
the null distribution is that of the reference simulations,
and \code{p.upper} is the p-value.
}
\description{
\code{print()} shows the result of \code{estimate_mrqap()} or of a test as a
report: how the null distribution was made, the estimates,
and the permutation p-values.

\code{tidy()} and \code{glance()} return the same results as a tibble, after
\code{{broom}}. \code{tidy()} gives one row for each term of a model, and
\code{glance()} gives one row for the whole model. They cover:
\itemize{
\item \code{netreg}, \code{netreg_lm}, \code{netreg_glm}, and \code{netreg_css},
from \code{estimate_mrqap()}
\item \code{network_test}, from \code{test_random()}, \code{test_configuration()},
\code{test_permutation()}, \code{test_gof()}, and \code{test_distributions()}
\item \code{netlm} and \code{netlogit}, from \code{net_regression()} in \code{{migraph}} before 2.0.0
\item \code{ergm}, from \code{ergm()} in \code{{ergm}}
\item \code{sienaFit}, from \code{siena07()} in \code{{RSiena}}
}

So an MRQAP result can sit in one table beside an ERGM or a SIENA one.
}
\details{
A permutation test gives a p-value but no standard error,
so the tidied regression results have no \code{std.error} column.
For a \code{netreg}, \code{p.value} is read from the permutations of the test
statistic, as \code{print()} shows it.
When the predictors are permuted, the intercept has no test,
and its \code{p.value} is \code{NA}.

For a \code{{migraph}} result, \code{conf.int = TRUE} adds the quantiles of the
permuted test statistics. These describe the null distribution,
not an interval around the estimate.
}
